<body>
<sec id="summary">
  <title>Summary</title>
  <p>Progress in modern natural language processing (NLP) is
  disproportionately concentrated in high-resource languages due to the
  abundance of digitized web-scale text. Low-resource languages, such as
  Sinhala (an Indo-Aryan language spoken by over 17 million people),
  suffer from severe data sparsity, fragmented digital corpora, and lack
  of standardized crowdsourcing infrastructure
  (<xref alt="Joshi et al., 2020" rid="ref-joshi2020state" ref-type="bibr">Joshi
  et al., 2020</xref>;
  <xref alt="Ranathunga et al., 2023" rid="ref-ranathunga2023sinhala" ref-type="bibr">Ranathunga
  et al., 2023</xref>).</p>
  <p><monospace>Sinhala-Stories-Dataset-Creator</monospace> is an
  open-source, fault-tolerant crowdsourcing and incremental dataset
  ingestion framework engineered for low-resource NLP. The framework
  decouples high-concurrency contributor submissions from large-scale
  corpus storage on the Hugging Face Hub, bypassing Git tree write
  contention and eliminating out-of-memory overhead during data
  ingestion. Although its flagship deployment curates the
  10.9-million-row <monospace>Isuru0x01/sinhala_stories</monospace>
  corpus, the framework is architecturally generalized: by modifying a
  single declarative configuration file
  (<monospace>config.yaml</monospace>), researchers and language
  communities can immediately deploy identical fault-tolerant ingestion
  pipelines for other low-resource scripts (e.g., Tamil, Dhivehi, Khmer,
  or Swahili).</p>
</sec>
<sec id="statement-of-need">
  <title>Statement of Need</title>
  <p>Collecting web-scale or domain-specific text for low-resource
  languages typically relies on participatory crowdsourcing
  (<xref alt="Nekoto et al., 2020" rid="ref-nekoto2020participatory" ref-type="bibr">Nekoto
  et al., 2020</xref>). However, deploying real-time crowdsourcing
  applications directly against version-controlled cloud repositories
  like the Hugging Face Hub
  (<xref alt="Lhoest et al., 2021" rid="ref-lhoest2021datasets" ref-type="bibr">Lhoest
  et al., 2021</xref>;
  <xref alt="Wolf et al., 2020" rid="ref-wolf2020transformers" ref-type="bibr">Wolf
  et al., 2020</xref>) presents severe engineering bottlenecks:</p>
  <list list-type="order">
    <list-item>
      <p><bold>Concurrent Write Collisions:</bold> Traditional
      Git-backed storage repositories fail when multiple distributed
      contributors attempt to commit records simultaneously, resulting
      in merge conflicts and dropped submissions.</p>
    </list-item>
    <list-item>
      <p><bold>Memory and Bandwidth Scalability:</bold> Directly
      appending records to massive multi-gigabyte monolithic Apache
      Parquet
      (<xref alt="Apache Arrow Developers, 2024" rid="ref-pyarrow2024" ref-type="bibr">Apache
      Arrow Developers, 2024</xref>) files in an active web app exhausts
      server memory and introduces unacceptable latency for end
      users.</p>
    </list-item>
    <list-item>
      <p><bold>Quality and Noise Gating:</bold> Low-resource web
      submissions often suffer from mixed-script contamination,
      synthetic text spam, and near-duplicate submissions. Running
      exhaustive pairwise duplicate comparisons across millions of
      records during web ingestion is computationally prohibitive
      (<inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[O(N)]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>O</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>N</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
      or <inline-formula><alternatives>
      <tex-math><![CDATA[O(N^2)]]></tex-math>
      <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>O</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:msup><mml:mi>N</mml:mi><mml:mn>2</mml:mn></mml:msup><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>).</p>
    </list-item>
    <list-item>
      <p><bold>Offline and Network Volatility:</bold> In regions with
      intermittent internet connectivity, contributors frequently lose
      long-form submissions due to transient connection drops.</p>
    </list-item>
  </list>
  <p><monospace>Sinhala-Stories-Dataset-Creator</monospace> resolves
  these challenges through a three-stage decoupled architecture
  featuring asynchronous staged micro-queues, automated two-tier
  deduplication, offline browser-side queueing, and scheduled
  incremental Parquet ingestion.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="state-of-the-field">
  <title>State of the Field</title>
  <p>Existing pipelines for low-resource language dataset acquisition
  generally fall into three categories, none of which address concurrent
  community crowdsourcing with automated cloud repository
  synchronization:</p>
  <list list-type="order">
    <list-item>
      <p><bold>Web-Scraped Corpus Extractors:</bold> Pipelines scraping
      Common Crawl or multilingual web dumps (e.g., CC100, OSCAR,
      MADLAD-400) suffer from catastrophic quality degradation in
      low-resource scripts, including machine-translated gibberish,
      heavy boilerplate contamination, and encoding corruption
      (<xref alt="Joshi et al., 2020" rid="ref-joshi2020state" ref-type="bibr">Joshi
      et al., 2020</xref>;
      <xref alt="Kreutzer et al., 2022" rid="ref-kreutzer2022quality" ref-type="bibr">Kreutzer
      et al., 2022</xref>).</p>
    </list-item>
    <list-item>
      <p><bold>Generic Survey and Form Tools:</bold> Form builders
      (e.g., Google Forms, Qualtrics, KoboToolbox) lack domain-specific
      validation, script ratio verification, cryptographic
      deduplication, and automated formatting into
      machine-learning-ready schemas.</p>
    </list-item>
    <list-item>
      <p><bold>Annotation Platforms:</bold> Tools such as Label Studio,
      Prodigy, or doccano focus primarily on human-in-the-loop
      classification and entity tagging for pre-existing texts, rather
      than large-scale, participatory raw text authoring and
      ingestion.</p>
    </list-item>
  </list>
  <sec id="build-vs.-contribute-justification">
    <title>Build vs. Contribute Justification</title>
    <p>Direct crowdsourcing interfaces hosted on Hugging Face Spaces
    typically attempt synchronous commits directly into the target
    dataset repository. Under active contributor traffic, this design
    suffers immediate Git lock contention, commit rejection, and severe
    memory exhaustion when re-serializing monolithic Parquet files.
    Rather than deploying and maintaining cost-prohibitive relational
    databases or distributed message queues (e.g., Kafka or RabbitMQ)
    that impose severe infrastructure costs on academic researchers,
    <monospace>Sinhala-Stories-Dataset-Creator</monospace> establishes a
    zero-cost serverless pattern. By combining Hugging Face Hub API
    primitives with GitHub Actions automation, it delivers
    conflict-free, cost-effective crowdsourcing accessible to
    under-resourced research groups.</p>
  </sec>
</sec>
<sec id="software-design">
  <title>Software Design</title>
  <p>The framework operates as a decoupled, three-tier ingestion and
  synchronization pipeline, illustrated in
  <xref alt="[fig:architecture]" rid="figU003Aarchitecture">[fig:architecture]</xref>.</p>
  <fig>
    <caption><p>Software Architecture: Three-tier decoupled
    crowdsourcing, staged micro-queues, and incremental Parquet
    ingestion
    pipeline.<styled-content id="figU003Aarchitecture"></styled-content></p></caption>
    <graphic mimetype="image" mime-subtype="png" xlink:href="figure1_architecture.png" />
  </fig>
  <sec id="key-architectural-components">
    <title>Key Architectural Components</title>
    <list list-type="order">
      <list-item>
        <p><bold>Client Submission Interface
        (<monospace>app.py</monospace>):</bold> Built on Streamlit, the
        contributor interface provides a responsive, low-latency
        environment for text input. The client executes real-time
        telemetry analytics (character counts, word counts, unique
        character counts, average word length, sentence/paragraph
        segmentation, and estimated reading time) and enforces
        contributor consent for open research release. If the remote
        Hugging Face API is unreachable due to network volatility, the
        client automatically diverts submissions to an encrypted local
        disk queue (<monospace>local_queue/</monospace>) and replays
        them upon connection restoration.</p>
      </list-item>
      <list-item>
        <p><bold>Conflict-Free Staging Micro-Queue:</bold> Rather than
        writing directly to the primary dataset file, each valid
        submission is serialized into an isolated micro-file
        (<monospace>pending/story_&lt;timestamp&gt;_&lt;hash_prefix&gt;.jsonl</monospace>)
        and committed via the Hugging Face Hub API. Because each
        submission targets a unique filename, hundreds of concurrent
        contributors can submit stories simultaneously without
        experiencing Git lock contention or merge collisions.</p>
      </list-item>
      <list-item>
        <p><bold>Automated Incremental Merge Engine
        (<monospace>merge_pending_into_main.py</monospace>):</bold> A
        scheduled GitHub Actions workflow (or daemon runner) executes
        periodically (e.g., every 5 minutes). The engine scans and
        downloads pending micro-files, harmonizes schema features,
        appends records directly to an auxiliary delta partition
        (<monospace>data/train-append.parquet</monospace>), updates
        SHA-256 fingerprints in <monospace>hashes.txt</monospace>,
        recalculates corpus statistics in
        <monospace>dataset_stats.json</monospace>, and deletes processed
        micro-files within a single atomic commit.</p>
      </list-item>
    </list>
  </sec>
  <sec id="engineering-design-trade-offs">
    <title>Engineering Design Trade-offs</title>
    <list list-type="bullet">
      <list-item>
        <p><bold>Staged Micro-Queuing vs. Centralized Relational
        Database:</bold> Choosing remote micro-file staging over an
        external database (e.g., PostgreSQL) eliminates hosting expenses
        and authentication overhead. The minor trade-off is eventual
        consistency, as submissions reside in
        <monospace>pending/</monospace> until the next scheduled batch
        merge.</p>
      </list-item>
      <list-item>
        <p><bold>Delta Parquet Partitioning vs. Monolithic File
        Appending:</bold> Appending records to an auxiliary delta
        partition (<monospace>train-append.parquet</monospace>) allows
        the merge engine to run within standard memory constraints
        (sub-500 MB RAM), completely bypassing the prohibitive network
        transfer and memory overhead of rewriting the 10.9M-row base
        corpus.</p>
      </list-item>
      <list-item>
        <p><bold>Two-Tier Deduplication vs. Exhaustive Pairwise
        Comparisons:</bold> Running pairwise n-gram or string distance
        comparisons against millions of records is computationally
        intractable (<inline-formula><alternatives>
        <tex-math><![CDATA[O(N)]]></tex-math>
        <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>O</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>N</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>)
        during real-time web requests. The framework trades full
        pairwise fuzzy matching across the historical archive for
        instant <inline-formula><alternatives>
        <tex-math><![CDATA[O(1)]]></tex-math>
        <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>O</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mn>1</mml:mn><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>
        SHA-256 cryptographic hash lookup across the full corpus,
        reserving fuzzy lexical Jaccard matching
        (<inline-formula><alternatives>
        <tex-math><![CDATA[J(A, B) \ge 0.85]]></tex-math>
        <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>J</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>A</mml:mi><mml:mo>,</mml:mo><mml:mi>B</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>≥</mml:mo><mml:mn>0.85</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>)
        strictly for the transient pending staging queue.</p>
      </list-item>
    </list>
  </sec>
</sec>
<sec id="quality-control-and-validation-pipeline">
  <title>Quality Control and Validation Pipeline</title>
  <p>The pure Python module <monospace>validator.py</monospace>
  decouples validation logic from the presentation layer to ensure
  modularity, high performance, and unit-testability.</p>
  <sec id="unicode-normalization-and-cleaning">
    <title>Unicode Normalization and Cleaning</title>
    <p>All input text is normalized to Unicode Normalization Form C
    (NFC) via <monospace>unicodedata.normalize()</monospace>,
    eliminating non-standard composable diacritic inconsistencies common
    in Indic and Brahmic scripts. Redundant punctuation (e.g., repeated
    dots <monospace>...</monospace>) is collapsed to single stops, and
    whitespace sequences are standardized.</p>
  </sec>
  <sec id="script-ratio-and-language-verification">
    <title>Script Ratio and Language Verification</title>
    <p>To prevent cross-language script contamination, the module
    calculates the ratio of alphabetic characters within the target
    Unicode block: <disp-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[\text{Ratio}_{\text{script}} = \frac{\sum_{c \in S_{\text{alpha}}} \mathbb{I}(U_{\text{start}} \le \text{ord}(c) \le U_{\text{end}})}{|S_{\text{alpha}}|}]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="block" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mtext mathvariant="normal">Ratio</mml:mtext><mml:mtext mathvariant="normal">script</mml:mtext></mml:msub><mml:mo>=</mml:mo><mml:mfrac><mml:mrow><mml:munder><mml:mo>∑</mml:mo><mml:mrow><mml:mi>c</mml:mi><mml:mo>∈</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>S</mml:mi><mml:mtext mathvariant="normal">alpha</mml:mtext></mml:msub></mml:mrow></mml:munder><mml:mi>𝕀</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>U</mml:mi><mml:mtext mathvariant="normal">start</mml:mtext></mml:msub><mml:mo>≤</mml:mo><mml:mtext mathvariant="normal">ord</mml:mtext><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mi>c</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>≤</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>U</mml:mi><mml:mtext mathvariant="normal">end</mml:mtext></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo></mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">|</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>S</mml:mi><mml:mtext mathvariant="normal">alpha</mml:mtext></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">|</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:mfrac></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></disp-formula>
    Submissions falling below the configured threshold
    (<inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[\text{Ratio}_{\text{script}} < 0.90]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:msub><mml:mtext mathvariant="normal">Ratio</mml:mtext><mml:mtext mathvariant="normal">script</mml:mtext></mml:msub><mml:mo>&lt;</mml:mo><mml:mn>0.90</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>)
    are rejected. Subsequently, <monospace>langid</monospace>
    (<xref alt="Lui &amp; Baldwin, 2012" rid="ref-lui2012langid" ref-type="bibr">Lui
    &amp; Baldwin, 2012</xref>) computes language identification
    probabilities using a normalized softmax over candidate
    log-likelihoods, ensuring high-confidence classification
    (<inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[P(\text{target}) \ge 0.90]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mi>P</mml:mi><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">(</mml:mo><mml:mtext mathvariant="normal">target</mml:mtext><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="postfix">)</mml:mo><mml:mo>≥</mml:mo><mml:mn>0.90</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>).</p>
  </sec>
  <sec id="heuristic-spam-and-synthetic-text-gating">
    <title>Heuristic Spam and Synthetic Text Gating</title>
    <p>A composite heuristic quality score penalizes abnormal repetition
    patterns: * Consecutive character repetitions of length
    <inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[\ge 5]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mo>≥</mml:mo><mml:mn>5</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>;
    * Repetitive consecutive n-grams; * High sentence-level duplication
    ratios; * Abnormal vocabulary diversity
    (<inline-formula><alternatives>
    <tex-math><![CDATA[\frac{|V_{\text{unique}}|}{|V_{\text{total}}|} < 0.40]]></tex-math>
    <mml:math display="inline" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mml:mrow><mml:mfrac><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">|</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>V</mml:mi><mml:mtext mathvariant="normal">unique</mml:mtext></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">|</mml:mo></mml:mrow><mml:mrow><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">|</mml:mo><mml:msub><mml:mi>V</mml:mi><mml:mtext mathvariant="normal">total</mml:mtext></mml:msub><mml:mo stretchy="false" form="prefix">|</mml:mo></mml:mrow></mml:mfrac><mml:mo>&lt;</mml:mo><mml:mn>0.40</mml:mn></mml:mrow></mml:math></alternatives></inline-formula>).
    Submissions with a quality score below 0.20 are flagged and
    prevented from entering the ingestion queue.</p>
  </sec>
</sec>
<sec id="research-impact-statement">
  <title>Research Impact Statement</title>
  <p>The primary deployment of this framework maintains the
  <monospace>Isuru0x01/sinhala_stories</monospace> corpus on the Hugging
  Face Hub, encompassing over 10.9 million rows (4.5+ billion
  characters) of validated Sinhala literature, folklore, and
  contemporary writing
  (<xref alt="Samarappulige et al., 2026" rid="ref-samarappulige2026zenodo" ref-type="bibr">Samarappulige
  et al., 2026</xref>).
  <xref alt="[fig:distribution]" rid="figU003Adistribution">[fig:distribution]</xref>
  shows the character length distribution of the dataset, highlighting
  broad coverage ranging from concise vignettes to extensive narrative
  chapters.</p>
  <fig>
    <caption><p>Length distribution of the 10.9M-row Sinhala stories
    corpus curated by the
    framework.<styled-content id="figU003Adistribution"></styled-content></p></caption>
    <graphic mimetype="image" mime-subtype="png" xlink:href="figure2_distribution.png" />
  </fig>
  <p>The corpus has been archived on Zenodo with persistent identifier
  <monospace>10.5281/zenodo.21195380</monospace>. Crucially, the dataset
  curated by this software directly powers downstream research in
  low-resource representation learning. Specifically, the corpus is
  utilized in experimental tokenizer research exploring
  akshara-preserving subword tokenizers for Sinhala large language
  models, evaluating token fertility, Akshara Integrity Rate (AIR), and
  byte-fallback frequencies against state-of-the-art multilingual
  baselines (e.g., LLaMA-3, Qwen-2.5, Gemma-2, and XLM-RoBERTa).
  Furthermore, the declarative design of
  <monospace>config.yaml</monospace> enables immediate adoption by
  research teams working on other low-resource Brahmic and African
  language varieties.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="ai-usage-disclosure">
  <title>AI Usage Disclosure</title>
  <p>Generative artificial intelligence tools (specifically Anthropic
  Claude and Google Gemini) were utilized during project development for
  drafting boilerplate unit test cases, refactoring modular components,
  and reviewing stylistic clarity in documentation. All core algorithmic
  designs, validation logic, system architecture, dataset curation
  workflows, and manuscript prose were conceived, implemented,
  empirically tested, and verified by the authors.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="acknowledgements">
  <title>Acknowledgements</title>
  <p>We express our gratitude to the contributors of the open-source
  libraries utilized in this project, including Streamlit, Hugging Face
  Hub, Datasets, PyArrow, NumPy
  (<xref alt="Walt et al., 2011" rid="ref-vanderwalt2011numpy" ref-type="bibr">Walt
  et al., 2011</xref>), and langid.py. We also thank the native-speaking
  contributors who contributed stories to the Sinhala open-access
  dataset.</p>
</sec>
</body>
<back>
<ref-list>
  <title></title>
  <ref id="ref-lui2012langid">
    <element-citation publication-type="article-journal">
      <person-group person-group-type="author">
